  "assumptions": [
    "Small swings: period T = 2π√(L/g) with g = 9.81 m/s², so a 4× longer string doubles the period (1 m gives 2.01 s).",
    "The string is light and doesn't stretch, and the bob counts as a single point; there is no air resistance or friction, so the swing never dies down.",
    "The swing angle is fixed at 12°. At that angle the real period is about 0.3% longer than the formula gives.",
    "The angle is computed from the timeline time, so changing the length makes the swing jump to a new point in its cycle. It then continues at the new period."
  ],
  "program": {
    "source": "const g=9.81,A=0.21;\nexport function evaluate({inputs,time}){const L=inputs.length;const T=2*Math.PI*Math.sqrt(L/g);const th=A*Math.cos(2*Math.PI*time/T);return{nodes:{swing:{quaternion:[0,0,Math.sin(th/2),Math.cos(th/2)]},str:{position:[0,-L/2,0],scale:[1,L,1]},bob:{position:[0,-L,0]}},readouts:{period:T,len:L}};}"
  },
  "timeline": {
    "duration": 60,
    "loop": true
  }
}